id:                     E6406
trigger_tool:           Conan|Meson|CTest|POSIX filesystem
phase:                  upstream-test|runtime-path-resolution|consumer-validation
error_signature:        PathConversion.SimpleTest expected canonical lowercase path but received the input case after normalized path_exists succeeded
severity:               2
category:               portability,filesystem,path-resolution,upstream-test,case-sensitivity,evidence
match_keywords:         PathConversion.SimpleTest, path_exists, to_native_path, case-insensitive POSIX, glob canonical spelling, uppercase path
title:                  POSIX path conversion must canonicalize case through directory enumeration

root_cause:             A POSIX implementation that returns immediately after path_exists() on the separator-normalized spelling cannot distinguish a case-insensitive lookup from an exact directory-entry spelling. On a case-insensitive POSIX mount, an input such as tests\\files\\PATHS\\EMPTY.TXT therefore returns the caller's uppercase spelling and bypasses the glob-based case reconstruction required by the API contract. The same implementation behaves correctly on a case-sensitive mount only because the normalized lookup fails and execution falls through to glob().
affected_scope:        Cross-platform C/C++ path converters that accept Windows separators on Unix-like systems and must return an existing path's case-preserving directory spelling; applies especially to HMDFS, network, and compatibility mounts with case-insensitive lookup semantics.
verification_cmd:      Exercise a real existing fixture with exact spelling, separator-only variation, case variation, and missing input on both case-sensitive and case-insensitive mounts. Require path_exists(to_native_path(input)), exact canonical spelling where the contract promises it, deterministic first-match ordering for ambiguous components, and an empty result for no match. Run the production upstream path-conversion test after the patch chain is applied.

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           canonical-glob-after-separator-normalization
rec_description:        Normalize separators first and always use the existing glob-based resolver on POSIX. Do not short-circuit on path_exists(posix_path), because that predicate reports reachability but not the directory entry's original case. Keep the direct unmodified-path behavior in the Windows implementation, where the API contract explicitly differs.
rec_command:            apply the patch to the POSIX resolver; run the focused upstream path conversion test and the complete package test command
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               fs_utils_posix.cpp removes the normalized path_exists early return and passes the normalized spelling to translate_to_glob_pattern()

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      filesystem-case-probe
fallback_description:   A mount-specific case-sensitivity probe may be used only when glob() is unavailable or a platform documents incompatible enumeration semantics. It must be frozen with the resolver and cannot infer canonical spelling from path_exists alone.
fallback_command:       use a task-local directory-entry enumeration with the same deterministic ordering and fail closed on ambiguity
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          platform-specific resolver adapter

forbid_1_pattern:       return the separator-normalized string as soon as path_exists(posix_path) is true on POSIX
forbid_1_reason:        Case-insensitive lookup makes reachability true for non-canonical spellings, so the shortcut loses the case-preserving directory entry.
forbid_1_consequence:   Upstream path conversion assertions fail on compatibility mounts, and downstream file opens may retain a spelling that is invalid after relocation to a case-sensitive filesystem.
forbid_2_pattern:       lower-case the complete input string or treat path_exists() as proof of canonical spelling
forbid_2_reason:        Case conversion is a filesystem property and directory components may use mixed or non-ASCII case; textual lower-casing is neither portable nor deterministic.
forbid_2_consequence:   Valid paths can be corrupted, ambiguous matches can be selected incorrectly, and a passing host-only smoke test can conceal target filesystem failures.

principles:             P005,P010,P013
causes:                 []
caused_by:              []
similar_to:             [E6405]
conflicts_with:         []
prerequisite_of:        []

